WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.868 --> 00:00:02.167
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:04.300
is an Emmy-nominated performer
and best-selling author.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:07.701
Please welcome back to the show
our friend Amy Sedaris.

00:00:07.701 --> 00:00:09.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:09.701 --> 00:00:16.000
♪♪

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.267
♪♪

00:00:22.267 --> 00:00:23.667
Welcome back.

00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:27.300
-Oh, wow!

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.167
Hi, Seth.

00:00:28.167 --> 00:00:30.701
-You look fantastic, as always.

00:00:30.701 --> 00:00:32.334
What a wonderful choice
you've made,

00:00:32.334 --> 00:00:35.434
your sartorial style unrivaled.

00:00:35.434 --> 00:00:37.801
-Well, I was a little limited
on what I could wear

00:00:37.801 --> 00:00:39.234
because John Early is --

00:00:39.234 --> 00:00:41.334
-The fantastic John Early,
wonderful comedian.

00:00:41.334 --> 00:00:42.767
-Well, I own
the apartment above me,

00:00:42.767 --> 00:00:44.667
and he's staying there,

00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:46.767
overstaying his visit
for like two weeks.

00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:49.267
But all my getting-ready dresses
are up there.

00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:50.667
So I was like,
I don't want to go up there

00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:51.868
when someone's staying up there

00:00:51.868 --> 00:00:55.133
because then I see
an unmade bed, underpants.

00:00:55.133 --> 00:00:58.133
-Oh, so you're not going
out of respect for them.

00:00:58.133 --> 00:00:59.501
You don't want
to spin yourself out.

00:00:59.501 --> 00:01:00.901
-I don't want to spin myself out

00:01:00.901 --> 00:01:04.167
and see an extra pair of shoes
that don't belong to him.

00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:07.434
So anyway, this is what I had.
But I love this dress.

00:01:07.434 --> 00:01:10.300
-So this isn't even in your
first choice line of dresses.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.434
-No. I've had this for five
years and I've never washed it.

00:01:12.434 --> 00:01:14.367
Shoo, shoo, shoo,
shoo, shoo, shoo!

00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:16.100
How dare you?

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.534
-He'll text me messages

00:01:17.534 --> 00:01:19.100
and say, "I crawled down
the fire escape

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.234
and watched you sleep
last night."

00:01:21.234 --> 00:01:22.601
-That's what your upstairs --
-Yeah.

00:01:22.601 --> 00:01:23.968
This is what John Early told me.

00:01:23.968 --> 00:01:26.067
Then he's like, "When I told
the doorman I was leaving,

00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:27.701
they burst into tears."

00:01:27.701 --> 00:01:29.601
And then I'll run into him
on the street,

00:01:29.601 --> 00:01:32.133
and it's 1:00 in the afternoon,
and he's like, "I just woke up."

00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:34.267
And I'm like,
"John, what is going on?"

00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:36.501
He's panfrying croissants
up there.

00:01:36.501 --> 00:01:38.133
-Oh, my God.
-But he's a good guest.

00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:39.267
-I don't know.

00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:40.434
It doesn't sound like
he's a good guest.

00:01:40.434 --> 00:01:41.801
-He is. He is. He is.

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:44.667
-Now, I like doing a check-in
every time you're here.

00:01:44.667 --> 00:01:47.534
about your dear rabbit...
-Tina Bunny.

00:01:47.534 --> 00:01:48.734
-...Tina Bunny.
-Yes.

00:01:48.734 --> 00:01:50.601
-Now, Tina Bunny
is a male rabbit.

00:01:50.601 --> 00:01:52.567
-Mm-hmm.
-But you have had --

00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:54.367
The first time you were
on the show nine years ago,

00:01:54.367 --> 00:01:55.400
we discussed Tina.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.234
-I brought Tina home that night,

00:01:57.234 --> 00:01:59.400
and then I was freaking out
that I made a mistake.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.701
This is my third rabbit.
They told me it was a girl.

00:02:01.701 --> 00:02:03.801
Then four years later,
they told me it was a boy.

00:02:03.801 --> 00:02:05.167
-Okay.
-Yeah.

00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:06.467
-So you are a fair--

00:02:06.467 --> 00:02:08.567
You consider yourself somewhat
of an expert on rabbits?

00:02:08.567 --> 00:02:09.934
-I am an expert.
I have a badge.

00:02:09.934 --> 00:02:11.901
I go to people's houses.
I educate them.

00:02:11.901 --> 00:02:14.667
-But it took you four years
to know that Tina was a boy.

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:15.934
-It's tough.
Yeah.

00:02:15.934 --> 00:02:17.567
They were trying to insert
a catheter and they said,

00:02:17.567 --> 00:02:20.367
"Amy, this is a boy bunny."

00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:21.968
-I will say, you cannot tell
from the photo, but this is --

00:02:21.968 --> 00:02:23.767
-Look at him.

00:02:23.767 --> 00:02:25.167
He's chewed a few things.

00:02:25.167 --> 00:02:26.234
-He has chewed a few things.

00:02:26.234 --> 00:02:28.801
But that is his right
as a rabbit.

00:02:28.801 --> 00:02:30.267
-Yes.
-Getting up there in age.

00:02:30.267 --> 00:02:31.968
How long does a rabbit go?

00:02:31.968 --> 00:02:33.734
-Well, my last rabbit
lived to be 12,

00:02:33.734 --> 00:02:35.000
which is old for a bunny.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.400
Tina's 10, but all of a sudden,
out of nowhere,

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.534
he started wetting my bed,
peeing in my bed.

00:02:39.534 --> 00:02:41.734
I don't know why.
I think it's behavioral.

00:02:41.734 --> 00:02:43.100
-You don't think it's age?

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:46.701
-No, because I have experience
with senior bunnies

00:02:46.701 --> 00:02:48.300
and I think
his kidneys are fine.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.334
But -- So I ordered -- I went
to a surgical supply store

00:02:51.334 --> 00:02:53.968
and I ordered a bunch
of those reusable medical,

00:02:53.968 --> 00:02:55.868
you know, bed pads, whatever.

00:02:55.868 --> 00:02:57.200
Yeah.
-Yeah. Okay.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.834
-But they arrived to my
apartment building unwrapped

00:02:59.834 --> 00:03:02.901
in just, like, saran that said,
"Amy Sedaris, Apartment 4C,"

00:03:02.901 --> 00:03:04.601
so everyone in my building

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:07.267
thinks I'm wetting my bed,
easily,

00:03:07.267 --> 00:03:10.267
'cause I'll be down in
the laundry room folding them.

00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:13.334
But anyway,
he stopped peeing in my bed.

00:03:13.334 --> 00:03:16.267
Now he just goes --
he just leaves little droppings.

00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:18.667
They look like peppercorns.
It's no big deal.

00:03:18.667 --> 00:03:19.901
So I safety-pin --

00:03:19.901 --> 00:03:22.701
If you have a pad
that's 48 by 48,

00:03:22.701 --> 00:03:24.300
that'll cover a queen-size bed.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:25.868
But they're hard to find.

00:03:25.868 --> 00:03:29.167
And so I safety-pinned it on top
of my, you know, bedspread

00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:30.434
so in case it happens.

00:03:30.434 --> 00:03:32.567
It happens between 2:30
and 3:00 in the morning.

00:03:32.567 --> 00:03:33.968
-Wow.
-Yeah.

00:03:33.968 --> 00:03:36.400
-He's just really dialed in
to that one certain window.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.501
-I don't know why.
I mean, I went through --

00:03:38.501 --> 00:03:39.968
During the strike, you know,

00:03:39.968 --> 00:03:42.100
I went through
a George Michael hole.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:43.968
I'm post-hole now,
but I was like --

00:03:43.968 --> 00:03:45.968
-George Michael the singer?
-Yes.

00:03:45.968 --> 00:03:47.434
-Yeah, okay.
-The "Wham!" documentary.

00:03:47.434 --> 00:03:49.701
Then I got obsessed with that.
Then I got obsessed with him.

00:03:49.701 --> 00:03:52.934
And maybe I was just so,
you know, obsessed with it

00:03:52.934 --> 00:03:56.567
that I was pulling focus from
my attention to Tina, maybe,

00:03:56.567 --> 00:03:58.000
and he was just getting even.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.601
-I see.
-My acupuncturist --

00:03:59.601 --> 00:04:03.934
-You're saying that maybe Tina
was wetting the bed on purpose

00:04:03.934 --> 00:04:05.200
because you were
too into George Michael?

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:06.634
-Maybe, maybe.
-Yeah.

00:04:06.634 --> 00:04:09.801
-And then my acupuncturist said,
"Well, did you ask Tina?

00:04:09.801 --> 00:04:11.667
Did you talk to Tina?"

00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:12.834
And I'm like, "Yes."

00:04:12.834 --> 00:04:14.734
And he's like,
"What did Tina say?"

00:04:14.734 --> 00:04:16.067
And I'm like, "Nothing."

00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:18.000
And I haven't been back
to that acupuncturist.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.601
-Yeah, that doesn't seem like

00:04:19.601 --> 00:04:23.200
it's an acupuncturist's
place to say.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.367
-Oh, my God.
So, yeah, that was something.

00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:26.767
-Did you buy or are you renting

00:04:26.767 --> 00:04:27.868
the apartment
next door to yours?

00:04:27.868 --> 00:04:30.033
-Okay, so then another apartment

00:04:30.033 --> 00:04:32.267
opened up in my building
right next door,

00:04:32.267 --> 00:04:34.267
and I thought, "Well, this
would be a good investment."

00:04:34.267 --> 00:04:36.167
So I just use it
as a storage unit.

00:04:36.167 --> 00:04:40.534
So I keep my hay in there,
luggage and petticoats.

00:04:40.534 --> 00:04:42.501
-Okay.
-But I do have Wi-Fi.

00:04:42.501 --> 00:04:43.767
-Well, that's great.
Yeah.

00:04:43.767 --> 00:04:47.033
-Fudge Pot. Fudge Pot's
the name of my Wi-Fi.

00:04:47.033 --> 00:04:48.501
-You're giving away your Wi-Fi?

00:04:48.501 --> 00:04:49.834
Oh, that's just the name
of the Wi-Fi.

00:04:49.834 --> 00:04:51.667
You're not giving out the
password, just the name of it.

00:04:51.667 --> 00:04:54.868
Fudge Pot and then do your best.

00:04:54.868 --> 00:04:57.000
So these are the sort of things
you would see over there.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.033
That's a small amount of hay.
This is the sort of --

00:04:59.033 --> 00:05:01.467
I guess it's good
for you to say there's Wi-Fi,

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:03.534
'cause I think if you walked
into an apartment full of hay,

00:05:03.534 --> 00:05:06.234
you might think it was
an Amish person.

00:05:06.234 --> 00:05:08.634
There might not be Wi-Fi
or a need for Wi-Fi.

00:05:08.634 --> 00:05:10.767
-It reeks of goats, trust me.

00:05:10.767 --> 00:05:12.334
But I can't imagine --

00:05:12.334 --> 00:05:14.601
I mean, to combine
my apartment --

00:05:14.601 --> 00:05:17.501
It's crazy to have
three apartments in one building

00:05:17.501 --> 00:05:19.300
and they're not attached,
but, you know,

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:22.734
the idea of having to, you know,
put a hole in my wall...

00:05:22.734 --> 00:05:24.334
You know, since my husband died,

00:05:24.334 --> 00:05:28.033
I don't have anybody who can
take care of that stuff for me.

00:05:28.033 --> 00:05:30.634
I don't want to think
about knobs and wallpaper.

00:05:30.634 --> 00:05:33.067
-Who wants to think
about knobs and wallpaper?

00:05:33.067 --> 00:05:35.234
But you're living
your best life.

00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:37.801
Knobs and wallpaper is just
going to slow you down.

00:05:37.801 --> 00:05:39.367
-It's crazy.
-Now, when your neighbors

00:05:39.367 --> 00:05:41.701
see giant boxes of hay
getting delivered,

00:05:41.701 --> 00:05:42.734
do you think they look at that

00:05:42.734 --> 00:05:45.767
the same way they look
at the pee pads?

00:05:45.767 --> 00:05:47.234
-That I'm a stoner.

00:05:47.234 --> 00:05:49.400
They probably think it's just
weed coming out of my house.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:50.534
Big boxes of weed.

00:05:50.534 --> 00:05:52.868
-Yeah, giant see-through
plastic bags of hay.

00:05:52.868 --> 00:05:54.467
-Well, you said
you had a goat before.

00:05:54.467 --> 00:05:55.834
You have goats?
-I've never had a goat.

00:05:55.834 --> 00:05:57.634
My father-in-law had a goat.

00:05:57.634 --> 00:05:58.734
-Same thing.
-Yeah, no.

00:05:58.734 --> 00:06:00.400
-Really?
-Yeah, I inherited it.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:03.767
It was part of my dowries
that I inherited.

00:06:03.767 --> 00:06:05.300
He was like, "Will you take
my daughter's hand?"

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:08.467
And I'm like,
"Not without the goat."

00:06:08.467 --> 00:06:09.901
-I always wanted a goat.

00:06:09.901 --> 00:06:11.767
-Yeah, I don't think you do.

00:06:11.767 --> 00:06:13.767
-No, I know, yeah.

00:06:13.767 --> 00:06:16.067
-You know, he misses
his goat a great deal

00:06:16.067 --> 00:06:17.300
and talks about it lovingly.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:19.467
I did not find the goat
had a lot of charisma.

00:06:19.467 --> 00:06:21.167
-What was the goat's name?
-Raisin.

00:06:21.167 --> 00:06:23.601
-[ Laughs ]
That's cute.

00:06:23.601 --> 00:06:25.601
-And based on what
they leave everywhere...

00:06:25.601 --> 00:06:26.834
-Yeah.
-Not a bad --

00:06:26.834 --> 00:06:28.200
Peppercorn would have been
a good name.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:30.567
-Peppercorns or capers.
It's no big deal.

00:06:30.567 --> 00:06:33.434
-Capers. Capers would be
a very funny name for a goat.

00:06:33.434 --> 00:06:35.834
-Oh, yeah. Capers.
It would be adorable.

00:06:35.834 --> 00:06:39.300
-Capers would be a great name
for a kid's book about goat --

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:41.267
a goat who pulled crimes.

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:43.067
-Oh, yes. Oh, yes.
-Or solved them.

00:06:43.067 --> 00:06:44.601
Capers the goat.
-Yes.

00:06:44.601 --> 00:06:46.434
-Yeah, and he'd be
a goat burglar.

00:06:46.434 --> 00:06:47.734
It would be like a cat burglar,

00:06:47.734 --> 00:06:49.300
but you would, like,
definitely hear them.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:50.467
-I would buy that book.
-Alright, great.

00:06:50.467 --> 00:06:51.567
Well, let's work on it together.

00:06:51.567 --> 00:06:52.868
I have a bunch more
questions for you.

00:06:52.868 --> 00:06:55.133
We'll be right back with more
from Amy Sedaris.

